 "standard_text": "Multiply and divide directed numbers; use the rules for signs; solve problems with directed numbers. (summary of the NERDC JSS 2 Mathematics curriculum)",
 "meta_desc": "Free JSS 2 maths worksheets on directed numbers: multiplying and dividing positive and negative numbers, squares of negative numbers and mixed calculations. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "Same signs: positive. Different signs: negative.",
 "key_sub": "(−3) × (−4) = 12",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>When you multiply or divide two numbers with the same sign, the answer is positive.</li><li>When the signs are different, the answer is negative.</li><li>(−5)² = 25, but −5² = −25.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>(−6) × 4</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Different signs</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: −24</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>(−36) ÷ (−9)</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Same signs</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 4</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Thinking two negatives make a negative</td><td>(−2) × (−3) = 6.</td></tr><tr><td>Mixing up −5² and (−5)²</td><td>Brackets matter.</td></tr><tr><td>Using the × rule for +</td><td>−3 + (−4) = −7.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
